WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:08.360
The hard assumption here is that you've watched Part 3, giving an intuitive walkthrough

00:08.360 --> 00:11.120
of the back propagation algorithm.

00:11.120 --> 00:14.880
Here we get a little bit more formal and dive into the relevant calculus.

00:14.880 --> 00:18.520
It's normal for this to be at least a little confusing so the mantra to regularly pause

00:18.520 --> 00:21.920
and ponder certainly applies as much here as anywhere else.

00:21.920 --> 00:26.560
Our main goal is to show how people in machine learning commonly think about the chain rule

00:26.560 --> 00:31.000
from calculus in the context of networks, which has kind of a different feel from how

00:31.000 --> 00:34.520
most introductory calculus courses approach the subject.

00:34.520 --> 00:40.400
For those of you uncomfortable with the relevant calculus, I do have a whole series on the topic.

00:40.400 --> 00:44.800
Let's just start off with an extremely simple network, one where each layer has a single

00:44.800 --> 00:46.320
neuron in it.

00:46.320 --> 00:51.240
So this particular network is determined by 3 weights and 3 biases, and our goal is

00:51.240 --> 00:55.600
to understand how sensitive the cost function is to these variables.

00:55.600 --> 00:59.240
That way we know which adjustments to those terms is going to cause the most efficient

00:59.240 --> 01:01.960
decrease to the cost function.

01:01.960 --> 01:06.000
And we're just going to focus on the connection between the last two neurons.

01:06.000 --> 01:10.640
Let's label the activation of that last neuron with a superscript L indicating which layer

01:10.640 --> 01:11.720
it's in.

01:11.720 --> 01:16.520
So the activation of the previous neuron is a L minus 1.

01:16.520 --> 01:20.160
These are not exponents, they're just a way of indexing what we're talking about since

01:20.160 --> 01:23.280
I want to save subscripts for different indices later on.

01:23.760 --> 01:27.960
Now let's say that the value we want this last activation to be for a given training

01:27.960 --> 01:32.960
example is y, for example, y might be 0 or 1.

01:32.960 --> 01:40.280
So the cost of this simple network for a single training example is AL minus y squared.

01:40.280 --> 01:46.080
We'll denote the cost of that one training example as C0.

01:46.080 --> 01:52.000
As a reminder, this last activation is determined by a weight, which I'm going to call WL,

01:52.000 --> 01:57.520
times the previous neuron's activation plus some bias, which I'll call BL.

01:57.520 --> 02:01.320
And then you pump that through some special nonlinear function like the sigmoid or a ray

02:01.320 --> 02:02.320
loop.

02:02.320 --> 02:05.400
It's actually going to make things easier for us if we give a special name to this weighted

02:05.400 --> 02:10.400
sum, like Z, with the same superscript as the relevant activations.

02:10.400 --> 02:14.880
So this is a lot of terms and a way that you might conceptualize it is that the weight,

02:14.880 --> 02:20.160
the previous action and the bias altogether are used to compute Z, which in turn lets

02:20.160 --> 02:27.320
us compute A, which finally, along with a constant Y, lets us compute the cost.

02:27.320 --> 02:32.920
And of course, AL minus 1 is influenced by its own weight and bias and such.

02:32.920 --> 02:35.720
But we're not going to focus on that right now.

02:35.720 --> 02:38.080
Now all of these are just numbers, right?

02:38.080 --> 02:41.920
And it can be nice to think of each one as having its own little number line.

02:41.920 --> 02:47.360
Our first goal is to understand how sensitive the cost function is to small changes in

02:47.360 --> 02:49.760
our weight, WL.

02:49.760 --> 02:55.680
Or phrase differently, what is the derivative of C with respect to WL?

02:55.680 --> 03:01.160
When you see this Dell W term, think of it as meaning some tiny nudge to W, like a change

03:01.160 --> 03:03.200
by 0.01.

03:03.200 --> 03:08.760
And think of this Dell C term as meaning whatever the resulting nudge to the cost is.

03:08.760 --> 03:11.160
What we want is their ratio.

03:11.160 --> 03:17.720
Conceptually, this tiny nudge to WL causes some nudge to ZL, which in turn causes some

03:17.720 --> 03:23.160
nudge to AL, which directly influences the cost.

03:23.160 --> 03:28.000
So we break things up by first looking at the ratio of a tiny change to ZL to this tiny

03:28.000 --> 03:33.840
change W, that is the derivative of ZL with respect to WL.

03:33.840 --> 03:38.800
Likewise you then consider the ratio of the change to AL to the tiny change in ZL that

03:38.800 --> 03:39.880
caused it.

03:39.880 --> 03:45.840
As well as the ratio between the final nudge to C and this intermediate nudge to AL.

03:45.840 --> 03:51.120
This right here is the chain rule, where multiplying together these three ratios gives us the

03:51.120 --> 03:57.280
sensitivity of C to small changes in WL.

03:57.280 --> 04:00.960
So on screen right now, there's kind of a lot of symbols, and take a moment to just

04:00.960 --> 04:03.720
make sure it's clear what they all are.

04:03.720 --> 04:07.400
Because now we're going to compute the relevant derivatives.

04:07.400 --> 04:14.440
The derivative of C with respect to AL works out to be 2 times AL minus Y. Notice this

04:14.440 --> 04:19.480
means that its size is proportional to the difference between the networks output and

04:19.480 --> 04:21.400
the thing that we want it to be.

04:21.400 --> 04:26.000
So if that output was very different, even slight changes stand to have a big impact on

04:26.000 --> 04:28.360
the final cost function.

04:28.360 --> 04:34.000
The derivative of AL with respect to ZL is just the derivative of our sigmoid function,

04:34.000 --> 04:37.360
or whatever non-linearity you choose to use.

04:37.360 --> 04:41.520
And the derivative of ZL with respect to WL?

04:41.520 --> 04:46.080
In this case comes out just to be AL minus 1.

04:46.080 --> 04:49.440
Now I don't know about you, but I think it's easy to get stuck head down in the formulas

04:49.440 --> 04:54.160
without taking a moment to sit back and remind yourself of what they all actually mean.

04:54.160 --> 04:58.840
In the case of this last derivative, the amount that that small nudge to the weight influenced

04:58.840 --> 05:03.120
the last layer depends on how strong the previous neuron is.

05:03.120 --> 05:09.280
Remember, this is where that neurons that fire together wire together idea comes in.

05:09.280 --> 05:14.800
And all of this is the derivative with respect to WL only of the cost for a specific single

05:14.800 --> 05:16.520
training example.

05:16.520 --> 05:20.880
Since the full cost function involves averaging together all those costs across many different

05:20.880 --> 05:26.240
training examples, its derivative requires averaging this expression that we found over

05:26.240 --> 05:28.480
all training examples.

05:28.480 --> 05:33.000
And of course that is just one component of the gradient vector, which itself is built

05:33.000 --> 05:37.560
up from the partial derivatives of the cost function with respect to all those weights

05:37.560 --> 05:40.760
and biases.

05:40.760 --> 05:44.200
But even though that's just one of the many partial derivatives we need, it's more than

05:44.200 --> 05:46.440
50% of the work.

05:46.440 --> 05:50.280
The sensitivity to the bias, for example, is almost identical.

05:50.280 --> 05:58.920
We just need to change out this del Z del W term for a del Z del B.

05:58.920 --> 06:04.680
And if you look at the relevant formula, that derivative comes out to be one.

06:04.680 --> 06:10.280
Also, and this is where the idea of propagating backwards comes in.

06:10.280 --> 06:16.440
You can see how sensitive this cost function is to the activation of the previous layer.

06:16.440 --> 06:21.320
Finally this initial derivative in the chain rule expression, the sensitivity of Z to

06:21.320 --> 06:26.600
the previous activation comes out to be the weight WL.

06:26.600 --> 06:30.080
And again, even though we're not going to be able to directly influence that previous

06:30.080 --> 06:33.200
layer activation, it's helpful to keep track of.

06:33.200 --> 06:38.680
Because now we can just keep iterating this same chain rule idea backwards to see how

06:38.680 --> 06:43.680
sensitive the cost function is to previous weights and previous biases.

06:43.680 --> 06:47.920
You might think that this is an overly simple example, since all layers just have one neuron,

06:47.920 --> 06:51.680
and that things are going to get exponentially more complicated for a real network.

06:51.680 --> 06:56.360
But honestly, not that much changes when we give the layers multiple neurons.

06:56.360 --> 06:59.320
Really it's just a few more indices to keep track of.

06:59.320 --> 07:03.600
Rather than the activation of a given layer simply being AL, it's also going to have

07:03.600 --> 07:07.880
a subscript indicating which neuron of that layer it is.

07:07.880 --> 07:15.280
Let's go ahead and use the letter K to index the layer L-1 and J to index the layer L.

07:15.280 --> 07:20.720
For the cost, again we look at what the desired output is, but this time we add up the squares

07:20.720 --> 07:26.120
of the differences between these last layer activations and the desired output.

07:26.120 --> 07:33.200
That is you take a sum over ALJ-YJ squared.

07:33.200 --> 07:36.480
Since there's a lot more weights, each one has to have a couple more indices to keep

07:36.480 --> 07:38.080
track of where it is.

07:38.080 --> 07:45.720
So let's call the weight of the edge connecting this Kth neuron to the Jth neuron, WLJK.

07:45.720 --> 07:49.480
Those indices might feel a little backwards at first, but it lines up with how you'd

07:49.480 --> 07:53.840
index the weight matrix that I talked about in the Part 1 video.

07:53.840 --> 07:58.400
Just as before, it's still nice to give a name to the relevant weighted sum, like Z,

07:58.400 --> 08:03.280
so that the activation of the last layer is just your special function, like the sigmoid,

08:03.280 --> 08:05.080
applied to Z.

08:05.080 --> 08:08.640
You can kind of see what I mean, right, where all of these are essentially the same equations

08:08.640 --> 08:11.760
that we had before in the one neuron per layer case.

08:11.760 --> 08:15.400
It's just that it looks a little more complicated.

08:15.400 --> 08:20.560
And indeed, the chain rule derivative expression, describing how sensitive the cost is to

08:20.560 --> 08:23.600
a specific weight, looks essentially the same.

08:23.600 --> 08:29.400
I'll leave it to you to pause and think about each of those terms if you want.

08:29.400 --> 08:33.960
What does change here, though, is the derivative of the cost with respect to one of the

08:33.960 --> 08:37.800
activations in the layer L minus 1.

08:37.800 --> 08:41.960
In this case, the difference is that the neuron influences the cost function through multiple

08:41.960 --> 08:44.720
different paths.

08:44.720 --> 08:51.120
That is, on the one hand, it influences AL0, which plays a role in the cost function,

08:51.120 --> 08:56.400
but it also has an influence on AL1, which also plays a role in the cost function, and

08:56.400 --> 09:00.280
you have to add those up.

09:00.280 --> 09:03.680
And that, well, that's pretty much it.

09:03.680 --> 09:07.920
Once you know how sensitive the cost function is to the activations in this second to last

09:07.920 --> 09:12.360
layer, you can just repeat the process for all the weights and biases feeding into that

09:12.360 --> 09:13.880
layer.

09:13.880 --> 09:15.400
So pat yourself on the back.

09:15.400 --> 09:20.440
If all of this makes sense, you have now looked deep into the heart of back propagation,

09:20.440 --> 09:23.680
the workhorse behind how neural networks learn.

09:23.680 --> 09:28.080
These chain rule expressions give you the derivatives that determine each component in

09:28.080 --> 09:32.960
the gradient that helps minimize the cost of the network by repeatedly stepping down

09:33.040 --> 09:35.040
hill.

09:35.040 --> 09:38.960
If you sit back and think about all that, this is a lot of layers of complexity to wrap

09:38.960 --> 09:40.120
your mind around.

09:40.120 --> 09:42.880
So don't worry if it takes time for your mind to digest it all.

